



🔐 AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM) – The Security Backbone

🔸1. IAM Users, Groups, Roles, and Policies

🧑‍💻 IAM Users
Represent a human identity (admin, developer, ops).
Use for interactive access (console or CLI with access keys).
Store credentials (password + MFA, access key/secret key).
Avoid assigning permissions directly to users.
👥 IAM Groups
Logical grouping of IAM users.
Assign permissions to groups instead of users.
Examples: DevelopersGroup, OpsTeam, BillingGroup.
🔄 IAM Roles
Identities that can be assumed by users, services, or AWS accounts.
Used for:
EC2 instance profiles
Cross-account access
Lambda execution roles
Federated identities
Roles have temporary credentials, no long-term secrets.
📄 IAM Policies
JSON documents defining what actions are allowed or denied.
Two types:
Identity-based (attached to users, groups, or roles)
Resource-based (attached directly to resources like S3, Lambda)
🔸2. Inline vs Managed Policies

Type	Inline Policy	Managed Policy
Ownership	Strictly attached to one identity	Reusable across multiple identities
Best for	Fine-tuned, single-use permissions	Broad, reusable permissions
Visibility	Not visible in IAM policy list	Visible, versioned, trackable
Recommended use	Rare exceptions	Use managed policies for consistency
🧠 Best Practice:
Use AWS managed policies or create custom managed policies and attach to roles/groups. Avoid inline unless very specific edge case.

🔸3. IAM Policy Evaluation Logic

✅ Evaluation Order:
Start with "Implicit Deny" – by default, everything is denied.
Check Identity-based and Resource-based policies
Apply Explicit Allows
Apply Explicit Denies – they override everything else.
🚫 Explicit Deny
Always wins — even if there’s an Allow, Deny takes precedence.
📏 Permission Boundaries
Optional policy used to limit maximum allowable permissions.
Especially useful for:
Delegated access
Automated IAM role creation
Third-party access control
🧪 Example Evaluation:
If a user is allowed s3:GetObject via one policy but denied s3:* via another, the explicit Deny overrides the Allow.

🔸4. IAM Best Practices (Security-First Approach)

✅ Enforce MFA for all privileged users (Admin, Billing, Root)
✅ Avoid Root account use after initial setup — create an IAM admin instead
✅ Use roles for programmatic access instead of long-term credentials
✅ Apply least privilege — give only what’s required
✅ Rotate access keys regularly; prefer temporary credentials (STS)
✅ Enable IAM Access Analyzer for external access validation
✅ Monitor IAM activity with CloudTrail + AWS Config
✅ Use SCPs with AWS Organizations for guardrails
🔸5. STS & Temporary Credentials

⏳ AWS STS (Security Token Service)
Used to generate temporary, limited-scope credentials.
Expires after a defined duration (15 min to 12 hours).
Used in:
Cross-account access
Federation (SAML, OIDC)
IAM role assumption
📌 Session Policies
Attached only at session level
Further restrict permissions even if role has broader access
🧠 Use Cases:
External apps assuming limited-time access
Federated users via SSO
Automated pipelines using assumed roles
🔸6. IAM Policy Simulator

Tool to test IAM policy behavior before deployment
Simulate:
Identity + Resource + Context conditions
Conflicts between policies
Service-level permissions
✅ Use for validating:

Cross-account roles
Deny overrides
Conditional keys
🔸7. Resource-based vs Identity-based Policies

Aspect	Identity-based	Resource-based
Attached To	IAM User, Group, or Role	AWS resource (e.g., S3 bucket, Lambda fn)
Cross-account?	Needs trust policy	Native support
Evaluation context	Who is performing the action	What resource allows
🧠 Common resources supporting resource-based policies:

S3
Lambda
SNS
SQS
API Gateway
EventBridge
🔸8. Service-linked Roles

Predefined roles automatically created by AWS services
Required for service operations like:
ECS (ECS service role)
Auto Scaling
CodePipeline
Lex, GuardDuty, Macie
🧠 Cannot modify trust policy or permissions; they are managed by AWS.

🔸9. Cross-account Access Using Roles

✅ Secure Pattern:
In Account A: Create a role with trust policy allowing Account B
In Account B: Assume role from Account A via CLI/SDK
{
  "Effect": "Allow",
  "Principal": {
    "AWS": "arn:aws:iam::ACCOUNT_B:root"
  },
  "Action": "sts:AssumeRole"
}
Use in:

Centralized logging
Central IAM control
Shared services
Dev → Prod transitions
🔸10. Federation with SAML, OIDC, IAM Identity Center (SSO)

Options:
SAML (Active Directory via ADFS, Okta, Azure AD)
OIDC (Login via Google, GitHub, etc.)
IAM Identity Center (formerly AWS SSO)
Use Case:
Enterprise users log in once to AD/AzureAD → federated to AWS via IAM roles.

IAM Identity Center Benefits:

Central identity access
Assign permissions to groups centrally
Integrated with AWS Organizations
🔸11. Fine-Grained Permissions with Resource-level Control

Examples:

S3: arn:aws:s3:::mybucket/*
DynamoDB: Limit access to a specific table or key
EC2: Tag-based control ("Condition": {"StringEquals": {"aws:ResourceTag/Owner": "DevTeam"}})
Advanced Patterns:

Attribute-based access control (ABAC) with tags
Condition-based access using IpAddress, DateGreaterThan, etc.
