✅ 1. IAM Policy Evaluation Logic
Core Principle: IAM uses a logic tree to determine whether access is granted.
⚙️ Evaluation Flow:

Implicit Deny: Everything is denied unless explicitly allowed.
Explicit Allow: If a matching allow is found, access is granted.
Explicit Deny: Overrides any allow — always wins.
✅ Example:

{
  "Effect": "Deny",
  "Action": "s3:*",
  "Resource": "*"
}
Even if another policy allows s3:GetObject, the above will override it.

💡Real Problem:

Someone complains access is blocked. You check policies and see an Allow — but they still fail. Root cause? A deny in SCP, session policy, or resource policy.

✅ 2. Least Privilege Model Across Environments
Definition: Grant only the minimum permissions required to perform a task.
🌍 Environment-based Access Models:

Environment	Approach
Dev	Broader, with IAM conditions
Stage	Restricted by tags or resource types
Prod	Extremely tight, only required services/actions
📌 Tools:

Policy Conditions:
Time-based access (DateGreaterThan)
IP address restrictions (IpAddress)
MFA enforcement (Bool: aws:MultiFactorAuthPresent)
Tag-based permissions for resource ownership control (ABAC)
✅ Example: Tag-enforced permission

"Condition": {
  "StringEquals": {
    "aws:RequestTag/Project": "Finance"
  }
}
✅ 3. IAM Roles for EC2, Lambda, ECS, CodeBuild
Attach execution roles to services so they can interact with AWS.
Service	IAM Role Type	Use Case
EC2	Instance Profile	Write to S3, call DynamoDB
Lambda	Execution Role	Invoke SNS, read Secrets Manager
ECS Task	Task Role / Execution Role	Pull ECR images, write logs
CodeBuild	Service Role	Clone Git repo, upload artifacts
✅ Admin Tip:

Use least privilege policies + trust policies:

"Principal": {
  "Service": "lambda.amazonaws.com"
}
✅ 4. Cross-Account Access using IAM Roles
🔁 Use Case:

Account A (Prod) wants to read S3 from Account B (Logs).

🧩 Solution:

In Account B, define a role:
{
  "Effect": "Allow",
  "Principal": {
    "AWS": "arn:aws:iam::ACCOUNT-A-ID:root"
  },
  "Action": "sts:AssumeRole"
}
In Account A, assume that role:
aws sts assume-role \
  --role-arn arn:aws:iam::ACCOUNT-B-ID:role/ReadLogsRole \
  --role-session-name CrossAccountSession
🔐 Best Practice: Use ExternalId or session tags for validation.

✅ 5. Federation via SAML, Cognito, IAM Identity Center (SSO)
🔑 Goal:

Let external users (employees, customers, partners) log into AWS without IAM users.

Federation Type	Use Case	Tool
SAML 2.0	Enterprise AD integration	ADFS, Okta, AzureAD
OIDC	Public identity providers	Google, GitHub
Cognito	Customer login to app	Mobile/web apps
IAM Identity Center	Centralized AWS access	Org-wide IAM mgmt
🧠 Example: AD user signs into Okta → assumes an IAM role in AWS using SAML → gets temp credentials.

✅ 6. Permission Boundaries (for Delegated Access)
Define a limit on the maximum permissions a role/user can ever get — no matter what.
🧩 Use Cases:

Delegate IAM creation to DevOps, but restrict max privilege.
Prevent roles from being created with admin access.
✅ Example:

"Version": "2012-10-17",
"Statement": [{
  "Effect": "Allow",
  "Action": "*",
  "Resource": "*"
}]
Even if someone attaches AdministratorAccess, the boundary limits what it really means.

🧠 Note: Permission boundaries don’t grant access — they only restrict.

✅ 7. Session Policies for Temporary Credentials
Attached during sts:assume-role — they restrict permissions only for that session.
🔐 Use Case:

Let a CI/CD job assume a role — but limit what it can do in that run.

aws sts assume-role \
  --role-arn arn:aws:iam::1234567890:role/Admin \
  --role-session-name DevBuild \
  --policy '{"Version":"2012-10-17","Statement":[{"Effect":"Deny",...}]}'
🧠 Enforces least privilege for temp sessions, especially useful with automation.

✅ 8. Fine-grained Permissions with Resource-Level Control
Control access down to exact resources, tags, or conditions.
🎯 Examples:

Only allow S3 write to one bucket
Only access DynamoDB table prod-users
Use conditions:
aws:RequestTag
aws:ResourceTag
aws:TagKeys
"Resource": "arn:aws:s3:::company-logs/*"
✅ Combine with ABAC: let IAM tags decide access.

✅ 9. Service-linked Roles – Use Cases
Automatically created roles tied to AWS services.
Service	Linked Role
ECS	AWSServiceRoleForECS
CodePipeline	AWSServiceRoleForCodePipeline
Macie	AWSServiceRoleForAmazonMacie
🔒 Cannot be deleted or manually modified unless the service is removed.

🧠 Used for: background tasks, event notifications, service scaling, etc.

✅ 10. IAM Access Analyzer – Real-Time Validation
Detects unintended external access to AWS resources.
📌 Use Case:

Your S3 bucket is mistakenly public
A role can be assumed cross-account
KMS key accessible by an unknown principal
🛠️ How it works:

Scans resource policies (S3, IAM roles, Lambda, KMS)
Shows findings like:
S3 bucket allows access from *
IAM role trust policy open to all AWS accounts
✅ Workflow:

Enable Access Analyzer in Region
Review findings
Auto-generate least privilege policies using findings
✅ 11. Securing Programmatic Access with IAM Roles and MFA
🛠️ Best Practices:

Never store static access keys in source code
Use EC2/Lambda roles or aws-vault to manage credentials
For admins: enforce MFA + role switching only
🔐 MFA Protected API Calls

Require MFA to perform sensitive actions like IAM updates or console login:
"Condition": {
  "Bool": {
    "aws:MultiFactorAuthPresent": "true"
  }
}
✅ Enforce via SCPs or identity policies in production accounts.

